"<div><div class=\" response-message\"></div></div><div><div class=\" response-message\"><h1>Path 1 Exploration</h1>\n<h2>Starting Point</h2>\n<p><strong>Step S0:</strong> Define the function f(x) = x^2 sin(x) and identify its components u(x) = x^2 and v(x) = sin(x) for differentiation using the product rule.</p>\n<p>$$f(x) = x^2 \\sin(x)$$</p>\n<hr />\n</div></div><div><div class=\" response-message\"><h3>Step S1: algebraic</h3>\n<p>Differentiate the individual components $u(x) = x^2$ and $v(x) = \\sin(x)$ with respect to $x$.</p>\n<p>$$u'(x) = \\frac{d}{dx}(x^2) = 2x \\text{ and } v'(x) = \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin(x)) = \\cos(x)$$</p>\n<p><em>Justification:</em> Apply the power rule for differentiation to $x^2$ and the standard trigonometric derivative for $\\sin(x)$.</p>\n</div></div><div><div class=\" response-message\"><h3>Step S2: substitution</h3>\n<p>Apply the product rule formula $f'(x) = u'(x)v(x) + u(x)v'(x)$ by substituting the identified components and their derivatives.</p>\n<p>$$f'(x) = (2x)\\sin(x) + (x^2)\\cos(x)$$</p>\n<p><em>Justification:</em> Substitute $u(x) = x^2$, $u'(x) = 2x$, $v(x) = \\sin(x)$, and $v'(x) = \\cos(x)$ into the product rule formula for differentiation.</p>\n</div></div><div><div class=\" response-message\"><p>✅ <strong>Goal Reached!</strong></p>\n<p>The goal was to calculate f'(x) using the product rule for the function f(x) = x^2 sin(x). The final derivative f'(x) = (2x)sin(x) + (x^2)cos(x) has been correctly calculated and expressed, matching the goal requirements.</p>\n</div></div><div><div class=\" completion-message response-message\"></div></div>"